        {
          "id": "124_cohorts_are_rated_and_every_species_block_is_the_same_104",
          "description": "THE STRUCTURAL CLAIM OF THIS RUNG, and no row count can make it: 5,534 rows is equally consistent with many other block splits. S10 selects 125 of the 164 candidates for every pollutant-process `pollutantprocessmodelyear` covers and NONE for the others, which costs nothing because a chained row's existence never consults `rt_isSelected`. 124 of the 125 THC candidates are rated: model year 2000 electricity has no `emissionratebyage` ageGroup-2099 row for fuel type 9 and is dropped, while 2001-2020 electricity are rated at exactly zero. Total Energy keeps all 125 -- `emissionrate` is not keyed by age group, so the row that killed THC's 2000 electricity cohort does not exist to be missed -- and that ONE cohort is the whole difference between the 125-row blocks (91, 90, 98) and the 124-row ones (1, 6). Each HC species and toxic block is 104, the 124 minus every electricity cohort, and they are the SAME 104 because all of them fail on the one missing `methanethcratio` row for fuel subtype 90. Ethanol (21) is the exception at 64: its two ratio paths cover gasoline and E85 and no diesel. 2,767 rate rows emit, which is what `n_outputRateRow` declares.\n\nWHAT IS ASSERTED is `cohortSurvivorCount` (125, the cohorts the output carries) and a sample of rate rows, not the per-block counts: a rated, surviving, emitting THC row (124), the selected-but-unrated MY 2000 electricity THC row (144), species rows that do not emit (2912, 3404, 616), and a species row that does not survive on its own rate (493). The per-pollutant-process selected and emitted counts, the 2,767 recount and the per-depth emission flags are gone from the model and are not asserted.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.0
          },
          "assertions": [
