        {
          "id": "the_two_HC_speciation_lookups_key_on_the_fuel_subtype_and_on_the_output_pollutant_process_and_serve_four_species",
          "description": "`HCSpeciationCalculator`'s two tables, and the two different things their keys do. `methanethcratio` is keyed by (processID, fuelSubtypeID, regClassID) and a model-year band, and it fires on EVERY rate row of this run because all twenty-six share process 1 -- what selects the methane and NMHC rows is `rt_isMethane` and `rt_isNMHC`, the two pollutants this document names here, because hcspeciation.rs names 5 and 79 as constants too; methane takes the ratio and NMHC its complement, out of the same row. `hcspeciation` is keyed by the OUTPUT pollutant-process, so joining a rate row's own `polProcessID` to it both finds the constants AND identifies which rows are NMOG and VOC rows: neither 80 nor 87 is written anywhere in this document, and the table carries 8001 and 8701 where `process-airtoxics`'s carried 8701 alone, so NMOG needed no new equation at all. Both lookups are INNER, and fuel subtype 90 is in neither table -- that single absence is every species block's twenty missing cohorts. `oxySpeciation` is 0 on all 272 rows, so `speciation_factor` never leaves its constant.\n\nONLY THE NMHC FLAG IS STILL ASSERTED (1 on the NMHC block, 0 on THC and VOC). The methane flag and the per-row methane ratios, speciation constants and presence flags were variables that are gone from the model, so the two lookups themselves are now checked only through the emitted methane, NMHC, NMOG and VOC cells.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.0
          },
          "assertions": [
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.rt_isNMHC",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 1.0,
              "coords": {
                "rate_rows": 2789
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.rt_isNMHC",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 0.0,
              "coords": {
                "rate_rows": 3281
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.rt_isNMHC",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 0.0,
              "coords": {
                "rate_rows": 1
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "the_E85_altTHC_branch_speciates_VOC_from_a_different_fuel_than_it_burns",
          "description": "adjust.rs:625-692 and hcspeciation.rs:715-760, and it is worth a factor of 3.85. On an ethanol cohort of model year 2001 or later, MOVES duplicates the THC block of the supplied E85 formulation, scales the copy by `altRatio / ratio` -- 1.037215919005 / 1.044099696019 = 0.99340697 at model year 2020 -- and speciates THAT into VOC using fuel subtype 12's ratios, while the emitted NMHC still comes from the ordinary block and subtype 51's ratio of 0.822. So the VOC multiplier on this cohort's THC is 0.99340697 x (1 - 0.338) x 0.974 = 0.6405369, where the ordinary path would have given (1 - 0.822) x 0.934 = 0.1662520. The model year 2000 ethanol cohort has no `altcriteriaratio` row and takes the ordinary path, and a branch that fired everywhere and a branch that fired nowhere would both be wrong and only one of them would look it.\n\nONLY ONE INTERMEDIATE IS STILL ASSERTED: the E10 speciation constant 0.974 on the alt-THC row. The five conditions, the two criteria ratios and the VOC factors were variables that are gone from the model, so the branch is now checked through the E85 VOC cells of MOVESOutput in `the_four_air_toxic_paths_and_the_worked_examples_reach_MOVESOutput`.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.0
          },
          "assertions": [
            {
              "variable": "Onroad.rt_speciationConstantE10",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 0.974,
              "coords": {
                "rate_rows": 3363
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "the_four_air_toxic_paths_and_the_worked_examples_reach_MOVESOutput",
          "description": "`AirToxicsCalculator`'s FOUR live paths and the end of the multiplicative chain. ATRatioGas1 keys on the emission's fuel FORMULATION and on an ageID that resolves the model year -- gasoline at ageID 40 takes 0.037092331652 and at ageID 0 takes 0.047131980205, out of two windows that partition the run's model years -- and ATRatioNonGas keys on the fuel SUBTYPE and a self-described model-year group. The two cover pollutants 20, 21, 24, 25, 26 and 27 and are disjoint on every emitted cohort, so the ratio is a sum of one term there; the sum is written because the Go APPENDS every path. Ethanol (21) is the one output BOTH reach, on disjoint fuel types -- gasoline through ATRatioGas1 and E85 through ATRatioNonGas -- which is why its block is 64 cohorts and not 104. The two paths this snapshot adds are minorHAPRatio, on the fuel subtype, for pollutants 40 to 46, and pahGasRatio, on the BLOCK's fuel type, for naphthalene gas (185). A zero RATIO and a missing one are different: 1,3-butadiene on gasoline model year 2020 has a row whose value is 0 and is emitted at 0, while every species on electricity has no row and is not emitted at all. The ratios themselves are no longer variables and are not asserted -- what is asserted is the two ageIDs that select the gasoline windows, and the emitted cells. Then twenty-five `MOVESOutput` cells, all four fuels, both day types and both ends of the model-year range, including the E85 rows the altTHC branch decides and the four cells that are exactly zero. `output_rows` is rate-major over the 2,767 emitting rate rows in blocks of two day types.",
